      body: "On 8 June 1971 the Hessian Landtag, on the state government's proposal, elected Willi Birkelbach as Hesse's data protection commissioner, the office created by the 1970 act; the Hessian authority describes him as the world's first data protection commissioner. From the start, anyone who believed a covered public body had mishandled their data could appeal to the commissioner (§ 11 of the 1970 act). His successor, the Frankfurt law professor Spiros Simitis, who had helped draft the 1970 act, held the office from 18 June 1975 to 22 October 1991.",
