      body: "Kaliya Hamlin (now Young), Drummond Reed, Doc Searls and Phil Windley hosted the first Internet Identity Workshop in Berkeley on 26–27 October 2005 to compare proposals for Internet-wide identity, from Microsoft's InfoCard to OpenID and i-names. The first day opened with Searls on 'the rise of the fully empowered customer'; the second ran as a participant-driven 'structured open space' at the Hillside Club, at Hamlin's urging. One breakout discussed 'Identity Rights Agreements' — ways for users to state their privacy preferences — and concluded that what was missing was a legal framework to make such agreements binding.",
