      contribution: "May, a physicist who had retired from Intel, wrote 'The Crypto Anarchist Manifesto' in mid-1988, handing it out at the Crypto '88 conference and that year's Hackers Conference; he read it at the September 1992 founding meeting of the cypherpunks and posted it to the list on 22 November 1992. With Eric Hughes he instigated the group's first meeting, and Wired called him its 'in-house theoretician'. His manifesto predicted anonymous, untraceable networked markets and acknowledged that they would also enable illicit trade, extortion and even 'abhorrent markets for assassinations'.",
      influence: "The Register's obituary credits the manifesto with defining 'the contours and flavour of the Cypherpunks list', whose legacies it lists as Bitcoin, WikiLeaks, peer-to-peer software and information markets.",
