      contribution: "With David Goldschlag and Michael Reed at the Naval Research Laboratory's Center for High Assurance Computer Systems, Syverson began work on onion routing in late 1995 and presented the first design at the Information Hiding Workshop in Cambridge in May 1996: traffic is wrapped in layers of encryption and relayed through proxies so that no one but the initiator's proxy knows more than the previous and next hop. In the early 2000s he worked with Roger Dingledine and Nick Mathewson on the second-generation design, Tor, published with them at USENIX Security in 2004. He has stressed that the aim was to separate identification from routing rather than to guarantee anonymity.",
      influence: "Onion routing, conceived at a US Navy laboratory, became the basis of Tor, which Syverson described in 2011 as 'a global onion routing network with about a half million users on any given day'.",
      thread: "Onion routing's design goal was that the network itself should not reveal who is talking to whom, leaving it to the communicating parties to decide when to identify themselves.",
