      influence: "Phil Windley, a co-founder of the Internet Identity Workshop, calls the Laws of Identity 'a foundational work in online identity', and colleagues credit them with giving Microsoft's identity work the mission of 'making user-centric identity viable and adopted'.",
      thread: "The laws argue on practical, not moral, grounds that 'a system that does not put users in control will – immediately or over time - be rejected by enough of them that it cannot become and remain a unifying technology' — the premise of every later user-held-terms design.",
